{How fatal it is to relax one's attention and play aimlessly comes out very
clearly in the following position from Round 9. Instead of continuing
purposefully with the attack by 31 Rg5, White played} 31. e4 {, allowing Black
just the time for a counter-attack by} Rbc8 32. Qb4 Rc2 33. Re3 Re8 34. Qd4
Rec8 35. Rd3 Rh2 36. d6 exd6 37. exd6 Rcc2 38. Rg1 Nf4 39. Nf2 Rhxf2 {"A case
of fiddling whilst Sutton Coldfield was burning." Source: BCM, May 1956, p129.
Comments by Harry Golombek.} 0-1

